WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.560
They have their own story.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.760
Some of them have been there so much
longer than we have been there

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.360
and will be there...
outlive all of us.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.560
You lose that sense of time
and you're suddenly surrounded

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.360
by all these new smells
and these sounds of the birds

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.600
and the sounds of those branches
when they creak.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.560
It just is like a whole amazing
thing for the senses.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.160
That's what I'm trying.
I'm trying to get that feeling of...

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.080
the mystery of it.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.520
Whenever I walk into
a woods, I always feel

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.520
that I'm entering into a different
pattern of time or something.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.120
the way light is chopped up
by the leaves.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.760
It feels like the moment itself
is chopped up a little bit

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.080
and lots of different moments
are almost held within the envelope

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.160
of that little forest
in quite a magical way.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.320
It's actually quite hard to envisage
an environment without trees

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.720
and their habitats and what they mean
and how they behave.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.360
It's a rich, creative world

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.040
and I can't think that anybody's life
is worse off for having trees in it.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.840
Part of improving it for wildlife
is increased number of trees.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.800
Because trees do quite useful things,
don't they?

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.720
Anyone got any ideas what trees do?

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.440
We as Muslims
believe we are custodians,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.840
caretakers of the Earth.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.600
So we're hoping that
by planting a tree,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.680
we will benefit the Earth and it will
preserve it for future generations.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.320
If a Muslim plants a tree,
and a bird, an animal or a child

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.720
benefits from it then it will be
classed as a charitable act.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.560
And it's an act that doesn't stop -

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.040
it can carry on
even till the hereafter.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:54.760
I suppose I connect trees
with memory really, and with time.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.920
And maybe even learning about time.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.400
A is for Apple. A is for Acorn.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.760
Maybe there's some dim
connection in my head

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.920
between trees and alphabets
and books and learning.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.720
I'm particularly interested
in the relationship

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.520
between people and trees.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.520
I know probably a few hundred
of the trees in this plantation

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.160
personally.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.640
I revisit the same tree and think,
"How are you today?

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.560
Are you springtime yet?

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.520
I want to know when the tree first
produces its first flowers.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.640
It's like a farmer having lambs.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.440
It's that same thrill
of seeing new life isn't it?

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:39.760
I have to try and get this right

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.400
'cause it's a really good quote
about nature.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.960
"Still, nature is the
fountain's head,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.040
the source from whence
all must originally spring

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.120
and should an artist continue his
practice without referring to nature

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.560
he must soon form a manner."

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.440
And that's what I was finding
was happening with my work.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.520
I was like, "Actually my work is
becoming incredibly mannered."

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.120
So I went into
the middle of the rainforest

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.760
and did a residency in the jungle.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.200
And didn't intend on it

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.920
becoming the whole
rest of my practice...

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.080
well not forever, but up until now.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.280
And just fell in love
with these landscapes.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.440
And I came home
and after being in this place

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.680
where my eyes were completely opened

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.920
to the twists of the branches
and the curls and the leaves,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.120
and when I was looking
at my own landscape

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.040
and all these woodlands around me,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.000
it was like I was looking at them
for the first time.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.760
And so it continued this
inspiration that I had to paint them.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.680
"It was Monday, Bank Holiday,
near the end of May,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.280
rough middle of the day,
year and of the country

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.320
if the country is England.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.400
Oak apple or oak and nettle day,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.360
axis of my year
thinly plotted as my tree -

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.040
the far end of our thinning garden,
in a border shaped to waver

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.640
like a child's drawing of sea.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.600
Its clock-handed Y
where the trunk parted ways,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.920
a first rung, that even just turned
seven I step onto,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.280
into the above,
this wobbly earth above earth.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.920
Wordlessly I knew then,
I'd later be gone,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.880
like possibly my tree has,
from that border,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.400
and my attention divided
thinly as the light,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.840
or is that time, through the
green-grey space I was sitting in

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.520
as I reached up for a branch,
or is it balance, or vantage?

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.960
On this tentative level. A story."

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.360
How did it feel to plant a tree?

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.080
Something to be proud of, yeah.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.280
Cool.

